<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chosroid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th–6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Khosroid dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chosroid dynasty was the royal house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> (Kartli) in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> from the early fourth century, when Mirian III took the throne, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> abolished the monarchy around 580. Of Iranian origin, it presided over Iberia's conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and included Vakhtang I Gorgasali, the traditional founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>. Branches of the family later ruled as presiding princes.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Chosroids, also called the Iberian Mihranids, were a branch of the Mihranids, one of the Seven Great Houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and distant kin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>; other branches were placed on the thrones of Gogarene and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gardman">Gardman</a>. Their rise to the thrones of Caucasia marked the Sasanian victory over what remained there of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsacid-dynasty">Arsacid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>. According to the Georgian Chronicles, Mirian III was installed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> by his father, called Chosroes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-king">Great King</a> of Iran, through his marriage to Abeshura, daughter of the last Arsacid king Aspacures I; another chronicle, the Conversion of Kartli, makes him the son of an otherwise unattested King Lev.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mirian fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, but after the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a> of 298 he drew close to Rome, and the missionary Nino converted him, his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a> and his household. Iran and Rome then contested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> deposed Sauromaces II in 361, the Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> restored him in 370, and by 380 Iran had reunited the kingdom under Aspacures III and taken tribute. Resistance to Iranian influence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> by the church and part of the nobility left the Georgian alphabet as its chief cultural legacy.</p><p>The kings stayed generally loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> until Vakhtang I Gorgasali turned towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in 482 and, allied with the Armenian prince Vahan Mamikonian, waged an unsuccessful revolt until his death in 522. The family then declined, an Iranian viceroy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> governing with the local princes. After the kingship was abolished, the senior line held Kakheti and the junior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guaramid-dynasty">Guaramids</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klarjeti">Klarjeti</a> and Javakheti. In 588 Guaram I revolted, received the Byzantine dignity of curopalates and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> as a presiding principate.</p><p>His son Stephanus I went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and in 627 the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, had Stephanus flayed alive and gave his office to the Chosroid Adarnase I of Kakheti. Stephanus II had to accept tribute to the Arab Caliphate. Archil, a saint of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a>, was martyred by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in 786. With the death of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javanshir">Juansher</a> around 807 the line died out; its lands in Kakheti passed to local noble families, and the Bagratids took the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chosroid dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Iberia was a hereditary monarchy, ruled in turn by dynasties that Georgian tradition calls the Pharnavazids, Arsacids and, from Mirian III, the <b>Chosroids</b>, although the lists of early kings are partly legendary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
